L = {}
L["dove"] = """After forty days of rain, the water covered the whole earth. Noah, his family, and all the animals had been shut inside the ark for months. The Bible says that God remembered Noah. That is the turning point of the whole story. God sent a wind across the earth, and the water began to go down.

The ark came to rest on the mountains of Ararat, in the seventh month. But the water still covered the land, and Noah could not see anything from the window. Another two months passed before the tops of the mountains were seen.

Then Noah did something careful. He opened the window and sent out a raven. It flew back and forth until the water dried up. Then he sent out a dove to see if the water had gone down from the ground. But the dove found no place to rest, and she came back to the ark. Noah put out his hand and took her in.

He waited seven more days and sent the dove out again. This time, in the evening, she came back with a fresh olive leaf in her beak. Noah knew that the water had gone down enough for trees to be growing again. The olive leaf is where we get the picture of a dove with an olive branch as a sign of peace.

He waited another seven days and sent the dove out a third time. This time she did not come back. The earth was dry enough for her to live on. Noah removed the covering of the ark and looked out. The ground was dry.

But he still did not leave. He waited until God told him. Finally God said, come out of the ark, you and your wife, your sons and their wives. Bring out every living thing. After more than a year inside, they stepped out onto dry land.

The first thing Noah did was to build an altar and offer a sacrifice to the Lord. He had gone through the greatest disaster the world has ever known, and the first thing he did was to give thanks. God smelled the pleasing aroma and said in his heart that he would never again curse the ground because of people, even though their hearts are evil from childhood.

Then God made a promise. He said he would never again destroy all life with a flood. He set his rainbow in the clouds as the sign of that promise. Whenever it appears, he said, I will see it and remember.

Why does this matter? The dove came back with something small and green. It was a sign of new life, and God's way of showing that the judgment was over. Noah learned to wait for God in both directions, waiting to leave the ark as well as waiting to be rescued.

The dove shows up again at another important moment. When Jesus was baptized, the Spirit of God came down on him like a dove. Both times it was a sign that something new was beginning.

What happened after? Noah planted a vineyard, and not long after, his story gets complicated. Even a family saved by God could still sin. The world was washed clean, but human hearts were not. That is why the story could not end here. It still needed a Savior.

What does this mean for us today? When you are in a long season of waiting, this story is for you. Noah spent more than a year in the ark. He didn't know exactly when it would be over. He sent out a bird and waited, and he sent out another one, and he waited some more.

It also teaches us to look for small signs. An olive leaf is not much. But it was enough for Noah to see that God was bringing back life. God often shows his help in small green things before we can see the whole picture.

That is why this story is so important. It tells us that God remembers us, even in the long silence. The same God who remembered Noah remembers you, and the rainbow and the dove are still there, to show us that his promises hold."""

L["babel"] = """After the flood, Noah's family grew into a large number of people. God had told them to fill the earth. But the people had a different idea. They all spoke one language, and they came to a plain in the land of Shinar, which is in what we now call Iraq, and they settled there.

They said to each other, come, let's make bricks and bake them thoroughly. They used brick for stone and tar for mortar. These were new tools, and they were proud of them. Then they said, let us build ourselves a city, with a tower that reaches to the heavens, so that we may make a name for ourselves. Otherwise we will be scattered over the face of the whole earth.

Look at what they were doing. They weren't just building a tall building. They wanted to make a name for themselves, and they wanted to stay together instead of spreading out the way God had told them to. They were building to get their security and their fame from themselves.

Archaeologists have found the ruins of huge stepped towers called ziggurats all over the ancient Middle East. They were temples built of mud brick, tall pyramids with steps up the side, and they were meant to give the gods a way to come down. The Bible doesn't say this was one of them. But the scene fits that world very well.

God came down to see the city and the tower. The Bible is almost funny here. The people built a tower that reached for the heavens, and God had to come down to see it. He said, if as one people speaking the same language they have begun to do this, then nothing they plan to do will be impossible for them. He wasn't afraid of them. He was stopping the damage before it grew.

So God confused their language. They suddenly couldn't understand each other. They stopped building the city, and they were scattered over the whole earth. The city was named Babel, which sounds like the Hebrew word for confused.

Why does this matter? It explains why the world is divided into so many peoples and languages. But it also shows what pride does. When people try to reach God by their own power, they end up confused, divided, and scattered. The more you try to make a name for yourself, the less you can understand the people next to you.

What happened after? Just after this story, the Bible shows God doing it the other way. He called a man named Abram and said, I will make your name great. Instead of people making a name for themselves, God gave a name as a gift. All the peoples on earth would be blessed through Abram.

Babel is also answered at Pentecost. After Jesus rose, his followers received the Holy Spirit in Jerusalem, and people from many nations each heard them in their own language. At Babel, language split people apart. At Pentecost, God used language to bring them together.

What does this mean for us today? We still build towers. We build careers, followings, and reputations, and we want to be known. There is nothing wrong with building. But the question is whose name we are building for.

The story is also hopeful. God did not leave the world confused. His plan from the start was to gather people from every nation and language back together. Revelation shows a crowd from every tribe and tongue worshiping together before his throne.

That is why this story is so important. It shows that we can't build our own way to God, so God came down to us. That is the whole Christmas story. In Jesus, God did what the tower could never do. He came down."""

L["three-angels"] = """Abraham was an old man when this story takes place. God had called him many years before to leave his home and go to a land he did not know. He had promised that Abraham would become a great nation and that all the families of the earth would be blessed through him. But Abraham and his wife Sarah were still waiting for a child. He was about ninety-nine years old and Sarah was ninety.

One hot afternoon, Abraham was sitting at the entrance of his tent near the great trees of Mamre. He looked up and saw three men standing nearby. In that world, a stranger in the desert might die without shelter, so welcoming visitors was a serious duty. Abraham ran to meet them and bowed to the ground. He begged them to stay for a meal.

He didn't just bring a snack. He told Sarah to hurry and bake bread from fine flour. He ran to the herd, chose a tender calf, and had it prepared. He brought curds and milk and the cooked meat, and he stood next to them under a tree while they ate. It was a lavish meal for a few strangers.

As the story goes on, we find out who the visitors really were. The Bible says that the Lord appeared to Abraham. One of the three is called the Lord himself, and the other two are called angels. Christians have long seen in this scene a picture of God coming to eat at a man's table.

They asked, where is your wife Sarah? Abraham said she was in the tent. Then the Lord said, I will surely return to you about this time next year, and Sarah your wife will have a son. Sarah was listening at the tent entrance. She laughed to herself. She was far past the age for having children.

The Lord asked Abraham why Sarah laughed. Is anything too hard for the Lord? Sarah was afraid and said she hadn't laughed. He said, yes you did. A year later she did have a son. They named him Isaac, which means he laughs. Her laugh of doubt became a laugh of joy.

Before the visitors left, the Lord said something else. He told Abraham about Sodom, a nearby city so evil that its cry had reached heaven. Abraham, who had learned to trust God, did something brave. He stood before the Lord and prayed for the city. If there are fifty righteous people there, will you spare it? He kept going down, to forty-five, forty, thirty, twenty, and finally ten. Each time God agreed.

Why does this matter? It shows that God keeps promises even when they seem impossible. It also shows what a friend of God looks like. Abraham welcomed strangers, and he argued with God for the sake of other people. The New Testament says, do not forget to show hospitality to strangers, because some people have shown hospitality to angels without knowing it.

What happened after? Sarah gave birth to Isaac just as promised. Through Isaac came Jacob, and through Jacob the twelve tribes of Israel, and through them came King David, and finally Jesus. All the families of the earth were blessed through Abraham, as God said.

What does this mean for us today? Maybe you have been waiting for something for a very long time, and it seems too late. Sarah was ninety and she laughed. God did not scold her for being human. He just asked a question that is still true. Is anything too hard for the Lord?

It also means we should open our doors. Abraham ran, he gave his best, and he stood with his guests while they ate. You never know what a simple act of welcome might become.

That is why this story is so important. It shows that the God of the Bible comes close, eats at our table, and turns our laughing doubt into laughing joy. He keeps his word, on his schedule, and it is worth the wait."""

L["sodom"] = """Abraham's nephew Lot had moved to the city of Sodom. When the herdsmen of the two men started fighting, Abraham let Lot choose any land he wanted. Lot looked out over the well-watered valley of the Jordan and picked it. He pitched his tents near Sodom, and over time he moved into the city. The Bible says the men of Sodom were wicked and were sinning greatly against the Lord.

When Abraham heard that God was about to judge the city, he prayed for it. He asked God not to destroy the righteous with the wicked. God agreed that even for ten righteous people he would spare the city. Abraham knew Lot was in there, and he was trying to save him.

That evening two angels came to Sodom. Lot was sitting at the city gate. He got up, bowed down, and urged them to come to his house, to wash their feet and stay the night. They said they would stay in the square. He insisted, and they came. He made them a meal.

Before they went to bed, the men of the city surrounded the house. Both young and old, from every part of the city. They demanded that Lot bring the visitors out so they could abuse them. The Bible says they meant to do violence to the guests. Lot went out and shut the door behind him. He begged them not to do this wicked thing. They threatened him, and they came close to breaking down the door. Then the angels pulled Lot inside and struck the men outside with blindness.

The angels told Lot, get out of here. Bring your wife, your sons, and your daughters. The Lord has sent us to destroy this place. Lot went and spoke to his sons-in-law, but they thought he was joking. At dawn, the angels urged him to hurry. Even then, the Bible says, Lot hesitated. So the angels grabbed his hand and his wife's and his two daughters' hands and led them out of the city, because the Lord was merciful to them.

One of them said, flee for your lives. Don't look back, and don't stop anywhere in the valley. Flee to the mountains or you will be swept away. Lot begged for a small town nearby, called Zoar, and God allowed it.

Then the Lord rained burning sulfur on Sodom and Gomorrah. He destroyed those cities and everything in the valley. But Lot's wife looked back, and she became a pillar of salt. Jesus told his followers to remember Lot's wife. Her heart was still in Sodom.

Abraham got up early the next morning and went to the place where he had stood before the Lord. He looked down toward Sodom and saw dense smoke rising from the land like smoke from a furnace. The Bible says that God remembered Abraham, and so he brought Lot out of the catastrophe.

Why does this matter? It shows that God is just, and that his patience is real but not endless. It also shows his mercy. The angels pulled Lot out by the hand. He was a flawed man who had made a poor choice, and still God saved him. Peter calls Lot a righteous man who was tormented by the lawless deeds he saw every day.

What happened after? Lot's life after this was sad. He ended up living in a cave with his daughters. Even so, his family line continued, and from it came Ruth the Moabite, who became an ancestor of King David and of Jesus. God's mercy reached farther than Lot's failures.

What does this mean for us today? Be careful what you move toward. Lot moved a little at a time, from looking at Sodom to pitching his tent near it to living in it. We are also shaped by the places we move toward.

And when God says to get out, don't hesitate, and don't look back. The angels had to take Lot by the hand. Some things we are tempted to go back to were never good for us.

That is why this story is so important. It shows God's holiness and his mercy together. He judges evil, and he rescues people who are barely hanging on. If you feel like Lot, standing there hesitating, remember that God took his hand."""

L["hagar"] = """Hagar was an Egyptian servant in Abraham's household. She belonged to Sarah, and she had no say in her own life. When Sarah went years without a child, she did what people did in those days. She gave Hagar to Abraham so he could have a child through her. Hagar became pregnant, and almost at once there was trouble in the house. Sarah was jealous and treated Hagar harshly, and Hagar ran away into the desert.

Out there by a spring on the road to Shur, the angel of the Lord found her. He called her by name and asked, Hagar, servant of Sarah, where have you come from and where are you going? It was the first time anyone in the Bible stories asked her a question. He told her to go back and gave her a promise. I will increase your descendants so much that they will be too numerous to count. You will have a son, and you shall name him Ishmael, which means God hears.

Hagar did something that no one else in the Bible does. She gave God a name. You are the God who sees me, she said, for she had seen the one who looked after her. The well was called Beer Lahai Roi, which means the well of the Living One who sees me.

Years later, Sarah had her own son, Isaac, at last. At the feast when Isaac was weaned, Sarah saw Ishmael, who was then a teenager, making fun of him. She told Abraham to get rid of Hagar and her son. Abraham was very upset, because Ishmael was his son. But God told him not to be troubled. Listen to Sarah, he said. I will make the son of the servant into a nation also, because he is your offspring.

Early the next morning, Abraham gave Hagar some bread and a skin of water. He put them on her shoulder and sent her away with the boy. She wandered in the wilderness of Beersheba. When the water ran out, she put the boy under a bush. Then she went and sat down about a bowshot away. She said, I cannot watch the boy die. And she began to sob.

God heard the boy crying. The angel of God called to Hagar from heaven. What is the matter, Hagar? Do not be afraid. God has heard the boy crying as he lies there. Lift him up and take him by the hand, for I will make him into a great nation. Then God opened her eyes and she saw a well of water. She filled the skin and gave the boy a drink.

God was with the boy as he grew up. He lived in the desert and became an archer. His mother got him a wife from Egypt. The Bible says that twelve princes came from Ishmael. Many people in the Middle East trace their family to him.

Why does this matter? Hagar was not a hero in anyone's eyes. She was a foreign slave, used and thrown out. Yet the God of the Bible stopped for her, twice. He is the only one in Genesis who talks to her as a person. It shows that God sees the people everyone else overlooks.

What happened after? Ishmael became the ancestor of the Arab peoples, according to long tradition. The Bible doesn't say exactly, but tradition has said that this is where their story begins. Paul also uses Hagar in a letter as a picture of a different kind of relationship with God, but her own story stands on its own.

What does this mean for us today? If you feel unseen, like nobody knows what you are going through, this story is for you. God's name for himself here is the God who sees me. He saw Hagar when she was a runaway and again when she was an outcast.

Notice too that he opened her eyes to a well that was already there. Sometimes the help is close, and we just can't see it through our tears.

That is why this story is so important. It shows that God hears the cry of the powerless, and that no one is too small for him to notice. When everything has run dry, he is the one who says, do not be afraid, I have heard."""
